C_PI_EXT pri_mb_xlat_pf_addr_error from speculative reads of CQ launch pages
==========================================================================

On GH200 (Grace, arm64) nodes with Cassini NICs, cxi_ss1 logs

  cxi_ss1 0020:01:00.0: cxi2[hsn3]: C_EC_INFO: C_PI_EXT error: pri_mb_xlat_pf_addr_error (12) ...
  cxi_ss1 0020:01:00.0: cxi2[hsn3]: C_EC_INFO: C_PI_ERR_INFO_RTRGT 0000700100250100 0ff0400000000420 0000000000000000
  cxi_ss1 0020:01:00.0: cxi2[hsn3]: C_EC_CRIT: C_PI_EXT error: pri_mb_xlat_comp_ca (19)

until the bits are auto-masked. The trigger is the CPU speculatively reading
command-queue launch pages, which cxi_user maps write-combined. On arm64,
write-combined is Normal Non-cacheable memory, which may be read
speculatively.


Environment
-----------

  GH200, kernel 6.4.0-150600.23.125-64kb (SLES 15 SP6, 64K pages)
  SHS 13.1.0: cray-cxi-driver 1.0.0, libcxi 1.0.2, libfabric 2.3.1
  Cassini firmware 1.5.61, links Gen4 x16, no AER errors


Decoding
--------

C_PI_ERR_INFO_RTRGT (union c_pi_err_info_rtrgt, cassini_user_defs.h) holds
the PCIe request the NIC could not decode. In every case seen it is:

  fmt/type   MRd, 64-bit address
  reqid      00:00.0 (CPU root port; not a GPU or other peer)
  dw_len     16 (64 bytes), first/last BE 0xf
  addr       BAR0 + offset inside a CQ launch page

C_MEMORG_CQ_LAUNCH: TXQ n launch page at BAR0 + n * 64K, TGT n at
BAR0 + 64M + n * 64K. On one production node (inference workload) 203 of
203 errors, on all four NICs and 34 different queues, were 64-byte CPU
reads of launch pages, at doorbell, low-latency and unused offsets
anywhere in the 64K page. Software only writes these pages.


Cause
-----

cxi_user_mmap.c, ucxi_mmap():

	if (static_branch_unlikely(&avoid_writecombine))
		vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_device(vma->vm_page_prot);
	else
		vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_writecombine(vma->vm_page_prot);

avoid_writecombine is enabled only when an Ampere Altra root port
(PCI_VENDOR_ID_AMPERE, 0xe100) is present (cxi_user_core.c, cass_cq.c).
On Grace launch pages are Normal-NC, so speculative loads and line fills
reach the NIC. Unchanged in release/shs-14.0.1.


Reproducer (reproducer/)
------------------------

wcprobe allocates a TXQ and a TGT queue with libcxi and touches the launch
page. run.sh builds it and runs one phase per NIC:

  # as root, idle node, error bits not masked
  cd reproducer && ./run.sh

Results (two runs, SHS 13.1.0):

  phase    access                                       pri_mb_xlat_pf_addr_error
  write    200k CQ NOOPs, doorbell + LL writes only     0
  prfm     PRFM hints, all lines of the page            0
  spec     mispredicted bounds check, no architectural  2 / 9 (TXQ), 10 (TGT),
           load from the launch page                    9 (whole page)
  read     one 8-byte or 64-byte load                   1 each

Each spec error decodes to the queue and line offsets the probe used, with
the same signature as the production errors. An 8-byte load is also
reported as dw_len 16 (line fill). Real loads returned normally: no SIGBUS,
SError or panic.


Fix test (patch/)
-----------------

patch/cxi_user_force_avoid_wc.patch is a test patch that enables the
existing avoid_writecombine branch of ucxi_mmap() through a runtime module
parameter. With the patched module loaded, spec gives 9 errors with the
parameter off, 0 with it on (TXQ, TGT, whole page), and 9 when it is turned
off again. With it on, a real 8-byte load is reported as dw_len 2, which
confirms the page is now mapped as Device memory. See patch/README.

This shows that the mapping attribute is what decides the error. It does
not say what the fix should be.


Notes
-----

- The driver logs about one occurrence per 50 ms per NIC, so logged counts
  are lower bounds.
- The "(was first error at S:NS)" value uses the NIC clock, which here was
  50-64 s behind the host.
- The cost of the device mapping on the low-latency command path was not
  measured.
- Kernel command queues (cass_cq.c, ioremap_wc) are not covered by the patch.
- Nothing was tested on SHS 14.0.1 (the relevant code is identical).
- No application impact of the error itself is established.


